    "narrative": "For Ghost Ride the WHIP today, we are leaning into high-upside speed and favorable matchups to navigate a volatile slate. **James Wood** (70.96) leads our charge as the highest-projected start; his leadoff role combined with a generous 4.27 xERA matchup against Bradley creates an elite ceiling for run production. Similarly, **Vinnie Pasquantino** (70.58) and **Corbin Carroll** (70.10) provide the core stability we require. **Corbin Carroll** continues to be our primary engine for Stolen Bases, and despite the moderate SP skill of the opposing pitcher, his dynamic platoon advantage ensures he remains a must-start staple in the OF2 slot. Meanwhile, **Trevor Story** (62.04) warrants special attention today; while his raw numbers are solid, the \"Slow SP Delivery\" variable attached to Valdez creates a high-leverage environment for **Trevor Story** to exploit the basepaths, making his score highly efficient relative to his batting position.\n\nIn the mid-tier, we are capitalizing on specific environmental boosts. **Seiya Suzuki** (68.45) is a prime example of an optimal situational play; he benefits from both a favorable park factor and a slow-delivering pitcher in Abbott, which bolsters his SB potential significantly. Conversely, our toughest bench decision involved **Alex Freeland** (40.41), who hovered just above the \"Below Floor\" threshold. He was ultimately edged out by the superior platoon and order-based projections of **Travis Bazzana** and **Cole Young**. We have opted to play it safe with **Jose Caballero** (39.32) on the bench today, as the matchup against a high-tier arm like deGrom combined with a bottom-of-the-order assignment suppressed his utility below our 40-point threshold. \n\nRisk management remains paramount today, particularly with **Tyler Soderstrom** (46.34) and **Austin Riley** (47.22). Both players occupy marginal territory; **Austin Riley** is struggling against the elite talent of Kirby, and while we are starting him for now, he is the first candidate to pull if we need to preserve game caps. **Mickey Moniak** is effectively neutralized by a rainout, removing any temptation to tinker with the roster late. With **Giancarlo Stanton**, **Brice Turang**, **Max Muncy**, **Luis Campusano**, and **Kyle Karros** all resting or off-roster for this specific calculation, the priority for the squad is maintaining efficiency through the top-tier hitters while avoiding the temptation to chase points from lower-floor assets like **Carson Williams**.",
